            <ArticleTitle>Esophagectomy After Weight-Reduction Surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>53-58</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1547-4127(17)30088-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.thorsurg.2017.08.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity is now epidemic worldwide, and an increasing number of patients have undergone a weight-loss procedure. Although obesity is a risk factor for esophageal cancer, there are few reports on esophagectomy after bariatric procedures. Careful understanding of the patient's gastroesophageal anatomy as a result of the bariatric procedure and attention to the creation of the esophageal replacement conduit are fundamental for the success of esophagectomy after bariatric surgery.</AbstractText>
